  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nLow level flow will become southwesterly ahead of a system\napproaching from the west today. This will lead to continued warm \nand dry conditions through the daylight hours today. Expect highs \ngenerally in the upper 60s with a few locations observing 70. \nThese readings will be close to 20 degrees above normal. Record \nhighs look safe today with values in the mid 70s. \n\nRecord Highs for 11/22 \nCVG 76 in 1913 \nDAY 74 in 1900 \nCMH 74 in 1931 \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nA weakening mid level trough axis will track across the area tonight,\nfollowed by a stronger mid level short wave that will move across \nthe Great Lakes region Monday. Shower activity will overspread our \narea from the west this evening and last only about 5-7 hours due to\nthe progressive nature of the system. \n\nGiven the weakening trend of the trough, and that it is progressive,\noverall QPF looks to be fairly light with most locations observing\nless than a tenth of an inch of rainfall. \n\nIn the wake of a cold frontal passage a colder airmass will settle \ninto the area later Sunday night and into the day on Monday. Highs \non Monday will only be in the mid 40s to lower 50s. The cold airmass \nwill then remain in place across the region through the rest of the \nweek, with daytime highs in the 40s and overnight lows mostly in the \nmid 20s to mid 30s.\n\nThere continues to be a fair amount of spread in model solutions for\nmid week with a southern stream system lifting northeast and \npossibly phasing with an approaching northern stream system. This \nwill likely lead to a chance of showers Wednesday into Thursday. \nPtype looks to be mainly rain, but thermal profiles may be just cool \nenough to allow for some wet snow to mix in at times.",
